 that's really honest about  its use of materials that it's  incredibly sculptural that it relates to  quite a lot of the art of the same  period and thus it's our architecture  that really wants to be kind of  imaginative and different London was  once a hotbed of Brutalism this is the  Royal National Theatre but fruit ilysm  was an international phenomenon the  aesthetics were radical and the social  agenda was too many of the buildings  have not aged well but if they're well  maintained and in a good location they  can be good places to live and the  Barbican estates here in the center of  London is a real example of how  brutalist architecture can work I mean  that's the private estate flats there  are very very expensive and people who  live there are incredibly enthusiastic  about it there is an online  international community of Brutalism  lovers the SOS Brutalism website posts a  growing database of buildings  the German architecture Museum in  Frankfurt is planning an exhibition in  Britain the Brutalism appreciation  society has 40 thousand followers on  Facebook bloggers in the US are also  singing the praises of the genre as a  script that supposedly Ralph knocks on  card it is easy to find it cool there is  a lot of positive chatter on the web  it's cold it is big and raw it speaks to  our instance perhaps to our baser  instincts Photography is after all  brutal it is one thing to be popular on  the Internet and another to be  acknowledged and appreciated by society  understood Stanley Kubrick's certainly  appreciated it as a dystopian nightmare  landscape in his 1971 film a Clockwork  Orange that could be considered a  contemporary critique nowadays brutalist  architecture has been tamed and  harnessed by old Couture its buildings  now figure as decoration on consumer  goods has a once bold vision decayed  into a lifestyle motif it becomes like a  black-and-white poster that you hang on  the wall and to show that you have good  taste but you have no connection to the  ideas and the philosophy that brought it  around and I think this is something  what a serious cultural shift that we  need to oppose admirers say brutalist  architecture deserves to be taken  seriously many hope the major show at  the German architecture Museum next year  quite helped to set the record straight  while talking of shows we finish off  today with the most glamorous show of  classic cars the Concours d'Elegance a--  it takes place each year at Lake Como  and has been running since 1929 and for  those who don't know it is the place to  be if you love classic cars movie stars  and a good old competition magnificent  cars from every era of motoring and  their proud owners  that's the concours d'elegance a-- a  kind of beauty pageant for automobiles  the event took place at Villa testet on  Lake Como one of the highlights this  year was the competition category cars  of the stars here vintage cars that once  belonged to movie stars vie for top  place like this ferrari 275 gtb this car  was delivered new in 1967 to Steve  McQueen the Hollywood legend it was  painted at the factory in a standard  factory color called not Yola which is a  hazelnut gold color which when steve saw  the cuy hated it so his preferred paint  shop at the time mixed a color dubbed  Chianti rad which was approved by Steve  McQueen had the sports car with a 300  horsepower engine kitted out according  to his taste  for instance aluminum spoke wheels and  he also transferred this unique but  quite useless rear view wing mirror he  was a one-off and he liked one offs he  liked things that were unique special  and the car was offered by RM Auctions  in Monterey California  two years ago we we bid on the car and  were lucky to be successful and brought  it to the UK this unique car costs on  twelve million euros a ferrari 275 gtb  is never cheap since only 280 were ever  made but the fact that Steve McQueen  once on this one raised its price to  four times what others usually cost it's  not nice to be out on the open road in  the McQueen for camp unfortunately we  won't be able to drive it quite as hard  as he used to drive it around California  the jury at the Concours d'Elegance a--  aren't impressed that an automobile was  once owned by a celebrity that doesn't  pertain to the judgment we got the cars  not to the history of teach cars  this is Marcello Mastroianni I don't  know it's difficult to say we feel is  more important than the other  this 1966 Ferrari 330 was once the  favorite car of Italian film legend  marchello Mastriani right next to it is  a Jaguar xk120 from 1952 it belonged to  American actor Clark Gable the king of  Hollywood some were motor sports  enthusiasts like Steve McQueen car gable  was more of an old-school Hollywood star  glamour was important had to be a little  sporty yet still suitable for the red  carpet each year the International Jet  Set meets at the Concours d'Elegance a--  Clark Gable would certainly feel at home  here but in the end the jury picked  another car  McQueen's Ferrari 275 gtb would Steve  have felt at home here too  oh absolutely absolutely who wouldn't  they wouldn't by the side of Lake Como  the beautiful Villa d'Este Hotel  surrounded by petrol heads and some of  the most beautiful cars ever produced  this year's concours d'elegance a--  benefited from a bit of Hollywood  celebrity and the cars on display were  just as glamorous as the films of  Hollywood's golden era  well that glamorous notice brought us to  the end of the show for today thanks for  tuning in and until next week take care  bye bye for now  you
 

